  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks to find a step function h(x) on [0,2] such that the maximum overlap integral C5 is minimized. The given initial solution achieves C\u22480.380897, but the target is to push this bound below 0.3808. The proposed improvements focus on overcoming the limitations of the current optimizer by:\n- Using grid doubling to increase resolution and allow for more precise representation\n- Implementing delayed projection to maintain numerical precision during optimization\n- Employing a tighter coupling between gradient-based optimizers and the smoothmax approximation\n- Using state recovery to ensure constraint satisfaction\nThe main bottleneck is balancing the high resolution needed for better accuracy with the computational expense of the optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nThe representation uses grid-based step functions with discrete optimization. The objective is:\nC5 = max_k \u27e8h, 1-h(k)\u27e9dx\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n(\u2211 h_i) dx = 1\nWhere \u27e8\u00b7,\u27e9 denotes discrete integration. The optimization uses a smoothmax approximation to handle the max constraint and projected gradient updates to enforce non-negativity.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n- Start from parent solution if available, or uniform initialization\n- Maintain a current resolution (default 400 points)\n- Use smoothmax approximation (exp(beta*(corr(k)-m)) to handle max operation\n- Perform projected gradient updates with delayed projection to preserve feasibility\n- Implement grid doubling by interpolation on a best candidate\n- Use adaptive optimization schedule based on performance\n- Include state recovery to re-enforce constraints\n- Track best solution and its resolution\n\n4. Search choices:\n- Initial temperature: 400.0\n- Projection threshold: 1e-8\n- Learning rate schedule: base=0.02, decay=0.99985\n- Grid doubling occurs after solving at a given resolution\n- Optimization parameters automatically adjust based on time\n- Use exponential smoothing to track progress\n\n5. Budget estimation:\n- Resolution increases: O(n^2) operations per optimization phase\n- Using numpy's optimized correlation and interpolation\n- Most costly step is the gradient computation\n- Overall complexity: O(T * n^2) where T is time budget\n\n6. Validity and validation:\n- Automatically verifies normalization after interpolation\n- Includes feasibility projection after each update\n- Tracks best solution irrespective of resolution\n- Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) directly\n- Implements state recovery to ensure solution validity",
